# Registered exemptions from the host-toolchain policy.
#
# docs/design/build.md section 0 is the policy; tests/host-toolchain-lint.sh is
# what enforces it; this is the list of invocations that break it today and the
# reason each one is still here. An exemption is allowed. An UNEXAMINED path is
# not, and neither is a waiver that outlives what it waived: the lint fails when
# a rule here matches nothing, so a renamed file or a fixed call site turns this
# file red rather than leaving a rule behind about something that is gone.
#
# Format: path<TAB>tool<TAB>reason. One row per (file, tool) pair, not per line.
#
# EVERY BUILD-SIDE ROW IS GONE. It held six when PLAN-080 section 5.5 opened it.
# RFCT-318 took three off by moving gen-dev-keys.sh and the two judges into
# pinned containers. PLAN-080 backlog B7 took the last three -- the two
# hack/check.sh scripts and the workflow that ran them -- by making
# .github/workflows/check.yml reach the Rust gate through tests/rust-gate.sh
# instead of a rustup toolchain it installed. Those scripts now declare
# themselves container-side at the top of each file, which they could not
# honestly do while one of their two callers was a bare host.
#
# WHAT IS LEFT IS NOT A BUILD, which is a different thing and is why the row
# below reads the way it does. PLAN-080 section 5.6 already named this
# category in prose -- rkdeveloptool flashing a board over USB, the dbus policy
# test, the QEMU suite's socat -- and this is the first time it has needed a row.
# A `mos-build-side: container` marker would be the wrong instrument: it claims
# the file runs in an IMAGE, and the lint says at its own head that it cannot
# check a declaration, so a false one is worse than a registered reason.
docs/bsp/cx3576-bench-collect.sh	rauc	NOT A BUILD, and not this repository's host. This script is copied ONTO A DEVICE and run there over the serial console -- its own header says "IT NEEDS NOTHING FROM THE REPOSITORY" and lists rauc among what a shipped root already carries. Line 497 is `rauc status --output-format=shell`, a READ of the booted slot by the qualification collector; it writes no artefact and PLAN-080 section 0.1's test puts it outside the boundary rather than inside it with a waiver. The row exists because the lint scans every tracked *.sh and has no marker for "runs on the device", and inventing one for a single file is a grammar nobody would maintain.
#
# Keep the file even when it empties again. The lint reads it when it is here
# and treats its absence as empty, so deleting it would change nothing that day
# and would remove the format documented above on the day somebody needs a row.
